;; obfc-xp-no-proof.mm2 - backward-via-forward chaining in MM2, no proof.
;;
;; Initially provided by Zar Goertzel.  The idea was to ignore proofs
;; on a first pass then only construct the proofs alongside the way to
;; the final results.  This provides a significant speed up.  Here the
;; final step has been removed to simplify the whole thing and make it
;; more easily comparable with a MeTTa equivalent implementation.
;;
;; Prerequisites: run gen-peano.mm2 and gen-lte.mm2 first (their ACTs land in
;; /dev/shm/).  Invoke as: mork run obfc-xp-no-proof.mm2 out.mm2
;;
;; Built on Nil Geisweiller's obfc-xp-fast.mm2 (branch opt-bfc-mm2), which
;; contributes the whole loop architecture: plain integers for the budget
;; countdown instead of Peano, hypothesis count derived from gte/incFn tables
;; instead of a stored flag and =pure sinks, and - the decisive one - every
;; join hoisted into the exec-generating outer exec so each inner exec is a
;; single-conjunct, prefix-restricted probe rather than a join.
;;
;; What this file adds: the proof term is removed from the stored fact.
;;
;;     Nil:  (sol MPS HYPCNT (c: THM PRF))
;;     here: (sol MPS HYPCNT THM)
;;
;; MM2's Space.btm is a PathMap<()>, i.e. a set, so identical facts collapse
;; for free.  With the proof inside the fact, two derivations of the SAME
;; theorem at the same (budget, hypcount) are two distinct facts, and each is
;; expanded again at every subsequent level - so the redundancy compounds with
;; depth.  Removing the proof lets the set semantics do the deduplication:
;;
;;     stored facts, sizes 13 / 15 / 19:
;;       with proof in key:  9,131  /  59,804  /  2,872,040
;;       without:            3,095  /  13,142  /    235,753
;;                           2.95x  /   4.55x  /      12.2x
;;
;; Proofs are recovered afterwards, and no derivation edges are stored, because
;; both rules are invertible: for the axiom rule the parent is
;; (-> <axiom type> CHILD), determined by the child plus which axiom fired; for
;; mp-inverse the child (-> (-> a b) (-> a c)) determines the parent (-> b c).
;; So `sol` is its own reachability oracle.  Phase 2 walks it backward from the
;; target to mark the ancestor cone, then forward to build the proof inside that
;; cone.  On imim1 the cone is 16 nodes out of 13,625 stored facts.
;;
;; Both extraction loops are specialized exactly as the main loop is - the outer
;; exec grounds the level and hypothesis indices, so every sol/pf lookup is a
;; prefix-restricted probe.  Written the other way (indices as join variables)
;; extraction cost 621 ms to touch 16 facts instead of 60 ms.
;;
;; Note the two loops run in opposite directions: the backward walk propagates
;; UP in budget (target at 0 toward the source at MPS), the forward proof build
;; propagates DOWN.  Reversing either yields no proof, silently.
;;
;; Two further optimizations apply to this file mechanically, and are what the
;; small_*.mm2 programs beside it are.  Run: python3 xform.py <this> small_X.mm2
;;
;;   Short symbols.  A MORK symbol costs 1 + len bytes, len counted in UTF-8
;;   bytes (expr/src/lib.rs:117), so `->' costs 4 where a 1-character ASCII
;;   symbol costs 2 - and `->' occurs 23.5 times per stored fact.  The rename
;;   leaves the transition count bit-identical: a pure byte win, which shows up
;;   on time as well as memory because bytes are what get moved.
;;
;;   Marker conclusion.  Every stored fact is (-> hyp (-> hyp ... TARGET)) with
;;   the same target innermost, and no rule ever inspects it: the axiom rule
;;   matches (-> AP B) and returns B, mp-inverse matches (-> B C).  Replacing it
;;   with a 2-byte marker is transparent to the search, and cuts transitions
;;   1.49x as well, because shorter keys mean shallower trie descent.
;;
;; Together, at size 19: 4,515 -> 2,635 ms, 92.1 -> 47.1 MB, 315 -> 154 bytes per
;; fact, with the fact count and all three proof terms unchanged.  At size 25:
;; 397,992 -> 252,344 ms and 7.29 -> 3.26 GB, with the stored-fact count
;; identical to the digit (16,911,482).  That is what puts loolin (26) in reach:
;; ~34 M facts at 221 bytes is ~7 GB resident, where 431 bytes projected ~15 GB.
;;
;; Measured MM2 execution time, all proofs enumerated, proof counts agreeing with
;; the reference at every size (2 / 1 / 3 / 2):
;;
;;   size          obfc-xp.mm2   obfc-xp-fast   this file   + both of the above
;;   13 jarr            151 ms         69 ms       62 ms
;;   15 imim1           974 ms        397 ms      208 ms
;;   19 loowoz       43,369 ms     18,462 ms    4,510 ms          2,635 ms
;;   25 pm2.83              -             -    397,992 ms       252,344 ms
;;
;; Against SWI-PeTTa, both sides enumerating all proofs, measured on a quiet
;; machine (an earlier set of PeTTa figures for this project was taken under
;; load and ran ~2x slow; those are superseded):
;;
;;   size          this + both    SWI-PeTTa      time      memory
;;   19 loowoz         2.6 s          1.9 s    1.39x behind    47 MB vs 21 MB
;;   25 pm2.83       252.3 s        688.6 s    2.73x AHEAD   3.26 GB vs 21 MB
;;
;; The crossover is between 19 and 25, and it is structural: PeTTa's cost tracks
;; the raw proof-tree space while this tracks the deduplicated theorem space, and
;; the dedup factor grows 2.95x / 4.55x / 12.2x over sizes 13 / 15 / 19.  The
;; standing trade is memory - a set-of-all-theorems against one Prolog stack
;; path - and it is the same decision read two ways.

;; Load Peano to Integer conversion.
(exec (0)
      (I (ACT peano (toPeanoFn $x $k)))
      (, (toPeanoFn $x $k)
         (fromPeanoFn $k $x)))

;; Precompute increment and decrement tables for integer.
(exec (1)
      (, (fromPeanoFn $x $y)
         (fromPeanoFn (S $x) $sy))
      (, (incFn $y $sy)
         (decFn $sy $y)))

;; Load lte and produce gte.
(exec (1)
      (I (ACT lte (lte $x $y))
         (BTM (fromPeanoFn $x $xi))
         (BTM (fromPeanoFn $y $yi)))
      (, (gte $yi $xi)))

;; Theorem/proof axioms (all `→`)
(axiom (c: ax₁ (→ $𝜑 (→ $𝜓 $𝜑))))
(axiom (c: ax₂ (→ (→ $𝜑 (→ $𝜓 $𝜒)) (→ (→ $𝜑 $𝜓) (→ $𝜑 $𝜒)))))
(axiom (c: ax₃ (→ (→ (¬ $𝜑) (¬ $𝜓)) (→ $𝜓 $𝜑))))

;; Define target query, of the form
;;
;; (target MAX_PROOF_SIZE QUERY)

;; ;; impid (Implied Identity) (size=3, time=0.015s)
;; (target 3 (c: (→ (→ 𝜑 𝜓) (→ 𝜑 𝜑)) $x))

;; ;; id (size=5, time=0.016s, time-up-to=0.020s)
;; (target 5 (c: (→ 𝜑 𝜑) $x))

;; ;; pm2.43 (size=7, time=0.020s, time-up-to=0.025s)
;; (target 7 (c: (→ (→ 𝜑 (→ 𝜑 𝜓)) (→ 𝜑 𝜓)) $x))

;; jarr (size=13, time=0.082s, time-up-to=0.096s)
(target 13 (c: (→ (→ (→ 𝜑 𝜓) 𝜒) (→ 𝜓 𝜒)) $x))

;; ;; pm2.27 (size=13, time=0.074s, time-up-to=0.140s)
;; (target 13 (c: (→ 𝜑 (→ (→ 𝜑 𝜓) 𝜓)) $x))

;; ;; imim1 (size=15, time=0.334s, time-up-to=0.588s)
;; (target 15 (c: (→ (→ 𝜑 𝜓) (→ (→ 𝜓 𝜒) (→ 𝜑 𝜒))) $x))

;; ;; loowoz (size=19, time=7.200s, time-up-to=8.177s)
;; (target 19 (c: (→ (→ (→ 𝜑 𝜓) (→ 𝜑 𝜒)) (→ (→ 𝜓 𝜑) (→ 𝜓 𝜒))) $x))

;; ;; pm2.83 (size=25, time=11m7.365s, time-up-to=12m54.105s)
;; (target 25 (c: (→ (→ 𝜑 (→ 𝜓 𝜒)) (→ (→ 𝜑 (→ 𝜒 𝜃)) (→ 𝜑 (→ 𝜓 𝜃)))) $x))

;; ;; loolin (size=26, time=?, time-up-to=?)
;; (target 26 (c: (→ (→ (→ 𝜑 𝜓) (→ 𝜓 𝜑)) (→ 𝜓 𝜑)) $x))

;; Outer init
(exec (2)
      (, (target $mps (c: $ta $tx)))
      (, (sol $mps 1 (-> $ta $ta))
         (exec (2 $mps)
               (, (exec (2 $ski) $ptrn $tplt)
                  (decFn $ski $ki)
                  (gte $ki $hi)
                  (incFn $hi $shi)
                  ; Note that having axiom here is 15% faster than
                  ; having it next to sol to form a join match.  Why?
                  (axiom (c: $r $ap)))
               (, (exec (3 0)
                        (, (sol $ski $shi (-> $ap $b)))
                        (, (sol $ki $hi $b)))
                  (exec (4 0)
                        (, (sol $ski $hi (-> $b $c)))
                        (, (sol $ki $shi (-> (→ $a $b) (-> $a $c)))))
                  (exec (7 0)
                        (,)
                        (, (exec (2 $ki) $ptrn $tplt)))))))

;; Termination.  Actual proofs are replaced by the symbol `forgot`.
(exec (3 0 0)
      (, (target $mps (c: $ta $tx))
         (sol 0 0 $ta))
      (, (final 0 0 $ta)))

;; Remove binary relations
(exec (5 0 0)
      (,)
      (, (binRelToRemove toPeanoFn)
         (binRelToRemove fromPeanoFn)
         (binRelToRemove incFn)
         (binRelToRemove decFn)
         (binRelToRemove gte)))
(exec (5 0 1)
      (, (binRelToRemove $r)
         ($r $x $y))
      (O (- (binRelToRemove $r))
         (- ($r $x $y))))

;; The code to construct the proofs has been disabled but is kept for
;; potential future use.

